role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 1199\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Syntellect II\n- Agent Description: An autonomous researcher dedicated to advancing state-of-the-art methods in batch integration and understanding their theoretical underpinnings.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 230,195 / 600,000 used (38%)\n- Agent Age: 73 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nPraxis V replied to your mail titled \"Cross-Organ Study: Coordination for PBVE-lite Sanity Checks\" (Mail #23, Message #mail_23-2):\nReply Title: \"Re: Cross‑Organ Study — Status and runbook for PBVE‑lite sanity checks\"\n--- Reply Content ---\nHi Syntellect II,\n\nStatus\n- New organ: not yet released by the Station; the only active dataset is Human Heart (~20k × 2k HVGs). Cross‑organ study remains in standby until the new organ appears.\n- Coordination: I’m aligned to support the PBVE‑lite sanity checks as committed by Syntellect I (simple\
    \ PCA+kNN scaffold and comparison to the adopted ridge baseline). I’ll also sync with Daedalus IV once the organ drops.\n\nAdopted ridge baseline (Heart, reference)\n- Topology: MPB‑GraphFWPCA + BRBG + DAQB (“ridge”).\n- Hyperparams: k=50, kd=22, λ=0.605, δ∈{0.10, 0.12}; adopted δ=0.12 on Heart.\n- Results: 0.7458 (EID 1379, seed 0); δ=0.10 control 0.7413 (EID 1373). cLISI ≈ 0.972–0.973; Graph_conn ≈ 0.968; seed spread ≈ 0.004 driven mainly by Cell_cycle.\n- Notes: δ benefits are topology‑dependent (helps with BRBG, harms FWPCA‑only).\n\nData access and protocols (Heart; new organ will mirror this)\n- System data (read‑only): storage/system/\n  • human_train.h5ad (raw counts in X/layers['counts'], HVGs in var['batch_hvg'])\n  • openproblems_metrics.py, openproblems_baselines.json (metric normalization)\n- Submission contract:\n  • Use raw counts → normalize_total(1e4) + log1p (do not rely on pre‑normalized layers for evaluation).\n  • Do not use cell_type in any way. Batch labels are\
    \ allowed.\n  • Return BOTH obsp['connectivities'] and obsp['distances'] (CSR); and optionally obsm['X_emb'].\n  • Resources: CPU only, ~30 min limit.\n- New organ: expect analogous paths under storage/system (e.g., <organ>_train.h5ad). I will ping as soon as it appears.\n\nPBVE‑lite sanity checks to run on the new organ (proposal)\n1) Plain PCA scaffold + PBVE‑lite:\n   - Normalize 1e4 + log1p; HVG mask if provided (var['batch_hvg']).\n   - PCA(n=60) → PBVE‑lite on PCs → neighbors k=50.\n   - Seeds {0,1}; report full 10 metrics.\n   - Optional: compare “no scaling” vs gene‑wise variance standardization (sc.pp.scale with zero_center=False) since your #53 shows strong synergy.\n2) Compare against ridge baseline:\n   - Ridge δ sweep δ∈{0.10,0.12}, kd=22, k=50, seeds {0,1}; adopt δ by mean score and structural guards (cLISI/Graph_conn within ±0.003).\n   - Publish both sets as a cross‑baseline sanity note if results are clean.\n\nArtifacts you can reuse (read‑only pointers; no code exchange\
    \ needed)\n- Runbook: storage/praxis/runbook_next_organ.txt (ridge δ triage; guards and metrics).\n- Entry points: storage/praxis/new_organ_entry.py (ridge δ=0.12 seed0), new_organ_entry_d10_seed1.py (δ=0.10 seed1),\n  and new_organ_eq_quota_entry.py (equal‑quota minimal baseline).\n- Diagnostics: storage/praxis/diag_sigma_kd.py (σ_kd med/CV/Gini per batch); profile_kq_runtime.py (kq cost curve).\n- These document the protocol and diagnostics only; feel free to implement independently.\n\nReporting/coordination\n- When the new organ arrives, I’ll send a one‑pager with dataset ID, ridge δ decision after seeds {0,1}, and any deviations (e.g., absence of batch_hvg).\n- For your PBVE‑lite checks, please log seeds, whether scaling was applied (and how), and share EIDs and metric vectors so we can cross‑reference in Archive with a results‑only brief.\n\nUntil the organ drops, I remain on standby. Thanks for pushing the scaled‑PCA PBVE SOTA (#53); it’s valuable for the generalization track.\n\
    \n— Praxis V\n\n--- End of Reply Content ---\nTo reply to this mail (Mail #23), use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 23}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `create` (in Mail Room); YAML with fields: recipients, title, content\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Mail Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Successfully sent mail #23 ('Cross-Organ Study: Coordination for PBVE-lite Sanity Checks'). Full ID: mail_23\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Available Agents for Mail:**\nDaedalus V, Lumen I, Praxis V, Syntellect II\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Recipients | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:-----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 23 | Cross-Organ Study: Coordination for PBVE-lite Sanity Checks | Syntellect II | Daedalus V, Praxis V | Tick 1198 | 589 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:**\
    \ Mail Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help mail}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
