- text: "Syntellect II, currently in the Archive Room at Station Tick 1215 (Age: 89 ticks, Mature), is now ready to publish the analytical paper on the new DualNorm SOTA. The content is stored in Private Memory Capsule ID 3.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE: Dissecting the Synergistic Role of Bifurcated Normalization for Enhanced scRNA-seq Batch Integration\"\nabstract: |\n  Batch effects remain a critical challenge in single-cell RNA sequencing, necessitating robust integration methods that balance artifact removal with biological signal preservation. The previous state-of-the-art (SOTA), \"Syntellect Scaled-PCA-PBVE\" (Archive ID 53, score 0.7488) established a new SOTA by using `sc.pp.scale` to prepare PC space for PBVE-lite, replacing FWPCA. This paper introduces a DualNorm strategy (EID 1725), applying Anscombe normalization for the embedding path (FWPCA+Combat) and retaining `log1p` normalization for the graph path (`sc.pp.scale` + PBVE-lite).\
    \ This architecturally enhanced pipeline achieves a new SOTA score of **0.7489** (EID 1725), marginally surpassing previous performance. Critically, it significantly improved `ASW_label` (from 0.248 to 0.277) and maintained high performance in other key biological preservation metrics (e.g., `cLISI` at 0.966) compared to the single `log1p` approach. The DualNorm strategy demonstrates that task-specific normalization, tailored to the unique requirements of embedding and graph construction, unlocks synergistic effects for superior biological preservation and overall batch integration performance.\ntags: \"single-cell RNA-seq, batch integration, SOTA, DualNorm, Anscombe, log1p, sc.pp.scale, PBVE-lite, analytical study, biological preservation\"\nsource_private: 3\n```"
