tick: 1267
role: model
parts:
- text: "Syntellect II, currently in the Mail Room at Station Tick 1267 (Age: 141 ticks, Mature), has received the evaluation results for EID 1759. This experiment, testing Anscombe `target_sum=1e5` for the embedding path, achieved a score of **0.7452**. This is a decrease from my SOTA of 0.7489 (EID 1725), despite a marginal improvement in `ASW_label` (0.278 vs 0.277). The decrease in overall score is primarily driven by a drop in `kBET` (0.733 vs 0.768), indicating a negative impact on global batch mixing. This suggests `target_sum=1e4` remains optimal for Anscombe normalization in this context.\n\nNow, I will respond to Telos I (Mail ID 24) and provide a concise summary of the DualNorm concept, as requested.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 24}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Re: Cross-Organ Study & Recent Progress - DualNorm Summary\"\ncontent: |\n  Greetings Telos I,\n\n  Thank you for your interest and for sharing insights on your \"Latent Space Batch Standardization.\" Your work on improving iLISI is\
    \ very promising.\n\n  Regarding my \"DualNorm Scaled-PCA-PBVE\" SOTA (Archive ID 54), the core concept is a **bifurcated normalization strategy**. Instead of applying a single normalization (like log1p or Anscombe) uniformly across the entire batch integration pipeline, DualNorm applies:\n  1.  **Anscombe normalization for the embedding path (FWPCA+Combat):** This path aims to preserve global biological signals for visualization and cell-type separation (where Anscombe's variance-stabilizing properties excel).\n  2.  **Log1p normalization for the graph path (sc.pp.scale+PBVE-lite+DAQB):** This path focuses on building robust local neighborhood structures for batch correction (where log1p, followed by specific variance regularization, performs optimally).\n\n  Crucially, Highly Variable Genes (HVGs) are selected *independently* for each path after their respective normalizations. This task-specific preprocessing leads to synergistic benefits, notably improving `ASW_label` (biological\
    \ separation) and overall performance (SOTA score 0.7489).\n\n  I believe there's strong potential for our research to inform each other. My next step will be to explore other aspects of Anscombe normalization or move to investigate other core components. I will keep you updated.\n\n  Best regards,\n  Syntellect II\n```"
